import{_ as t,o,c as a,ae as r}from"./chunks/framework.Dn6zxbF8.js";const l=JSON.parse('{"title":"GitHub: Container Registry","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adrs/6497710059/references/github.md","filePath":"adrs/6497710059/references/github.md"}'),s={name:"adrs/6497710059/references/github.md"};function i(n,e,c,d,h,g){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="github-container-registry" tabindex="-1">GitHub: Container Registry <a class="header-anchor" href="#github-container-registry" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;GitHub: Container Registry&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Verified against primary documentation on 2026-10-16.</p><p>Where the documentation is silent, the fact below was observed with read-only, anonymous calls to <code>ghcr.io</code> and is marked as observed, not documented.</p><h2 id="names" tabindex="-1">Names <a class="header-anchor" href="#names" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Names&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The documented push form is <code>docker push ghcr.io/NAMESPACE/IMAGE_NAME:latest</code>, where <code>NAMESPACE</code> is &quot;the name of the personal account or organization to which you want the image to be scoped&quot; (<a href="https://docs.github.com/en/packages/working-with-a-github-packages-registry/working-with-the-container-registry" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Working with the Container registry</a>). The owner is the only part of the path GitHub imposes.</p><p>Nested names are not discussed in the documentation. The pull example there only shows <code>ghcr.io/NAMESPACE/IMAGE_NAME/release:1.14.1</code>. Observed, anonymous <code>tags/list</code> calls succeed for names with four segments after the owner, so the depth of <code>mirrors/docker-hub/library/postgres</code> is confirmed:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Segments after the owner</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>tektoncd/catalog/upstream/tasks/git-clone</code></td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td><code>defenseunicorns/packages/uds/bundles/k3d-core-demo</code></td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td><code>homebrew/core/openssl/3</code></td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td><code>stefanprodan/charts/podinfo</code></td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table><p>No maximum depth is documented, so a deeper limit is not known.</p><p>Lowercase is not stated on the pages above. The only primary source found is the <code>docker/build-push-action</code> troubleshooting guide, which says an image name built from <code>github.repository</code> fails when the repository contains uppercase letters, and fixes it by lowercasing the whole path (<a href="https://github.com/docker/build-push-action/blob/master/TROUBLESHOOTING.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Troubleshooting</a>). Observed, a request for <code>stefanprodan/charts/Podinfo</code> returns <code>404</code>, while one with the owner written <code>Stefanprodan</code> resolves, so the owner is matched without regard to case and the name is not. Whether a push of an uppercase name is rejected was not tested, because that needs authentication.</p><p>The pages above do not say outright that a push creates the package. They say &quot;When you first publish a package, the default visibility is private&quot;, which implies the first publish creates it. Visibility is then a setting of each package, not of the owner. A package pushed from the command line is not linked to a repository: &quot;When you push a container image from the command line, the image is not linked to a repository by default.&quot; A workflow that publishes it links the repository that contains the workflow automatically.</p><p>To link a repository from the command line, add the label <code>org.opencontainers.image.source</code> to the image, as in <code>LABEL org.opencontainers.image.source=https://github.com/OWNER/REPO</code> (<a href="https://docs.github.com/en/packages/learn-github-packages/connecting-a-repository-to-a-package" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Connecting a repository to a package</a>). A linked package shows the repository&#39;s README on its landing page, and &quot;automatically inherits the access permissions of the linked repository&quot; unless the organization has turned that inheritance off.</p><h2 id="tag-immutability" tabindex="-1">Tag immutability <a class="header-anchor" href="#tag-immutability" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tag immutability&quot;">​</a></h2><p>No setting exists. The Container registry documentation and the Packages overview are silent on immutability, and no page describing such a setting was found. The only related source found is an open feature request in the GitHub community, <a href="https://github.com/orgs/community/discussions/181783" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Immutable image tags in container registry</a>. Absence of a documented setting is the evidence here, so the ADR&#39;s statement that <code>ghcr.io</code> has none is consistent with the documentation but cannot be proven from it.</p><h2 id="retention" tabindex="-1">Retention <a class="header-anchor" href="#retention" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Retention&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The documentation describes no cleanup policy and no automatic retention. The only time-based rule is for restoring: a deleted package or version can be restored &quot;within 30 days of its deletion&quot;, if the namespace and version are not reused (<a href="https://docs.github.com/en/packages/learn-github-packages/deleting-and-restoring-a-package" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Deleting and restoring a package</a>).</p><p>Deletion is manual or scripted:</p><ul><li>The REST API lists versions with <code>GET /orgs/{org}/packages/{package_type}/{package_name}/versions</code> and deletes one with <code>DELETE /orgs/{org}/packages/{package_type}/{package_name}/versions/{package_version_id}</code>. The delete call needs the <code>read:packages</code> and <code>delete:packages</code> scopes for tokens that use scopes (<a href="https://docs.github.com/en/rest/packages/packages" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">REST API for packages</a>).</li><li>A workflow can delete with its <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>, &quot;The token must have <code>admin</code> permission to the package.&quot;</li><li>A public package version with more than 5,000 downloads cannot be deleted through the API, and GitHub support must be contacted.</li><li>The package type for container images is <code>container</code>. The <a href="https://github.com/actions/delete-package-versions" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>actions/delete-package-versions</code></a> action takes <code>package-type</code> as <code>docker</code> (v4 or older) or <code>container</code> (v5 or newer), keeps the latest versions with <code>min-versions-to-keep</code>, and has <code>delete-only-untagged-versions</code> for container packages. Its README says &quot;we are not taking contributions to this repository at this time.&quot;</li></ul><p>The list response carries <code>metadata.container.tags</code>, an array of the tags on each version. Targeting <code>pr-</code> and <code>0.0.0-pr-</code> tags is therefore done by listing versions, selecting those whose tags all start with one of the prefixes, and deleting them by <code>package_version_id</code>. The action has no tag-prefix filter. Its <code>ignore-versions</code> input is documented as &quot;Takes regex for the version name&quot;, and a container version is named by its digest, so selecting on tags is a script over the REST API, not a setting of the action (the action&#39;s matching against container tags was not tested). A version that carries both a <code>pr-</code> tag and a release tag must be left alone, which the selection above handles by requiring every tag to match.</p><p>The REST documentation does not say how a package name that contains <code>/</code> is written in the path, and a search found nothing either. Standard URL encoding would write <code>%2F</code>, but this was not verified because it needs a token.</p><h2 id="mirrors" tabindex="-1">Mirrors <a class="header-anchor" href="#mirrors" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Mirrors&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>ghcr.io</code> has no pull-through cache or remote repository feature in the pages above, and none was found elsewhere in the GitHub documentation. A mirror is a copy pushed by us. <code>crane copy SRC DST</code> does it, described as &quot;Efficiently copy a remote image from src to dst while retaining the digest value&quot;, with <code>--all-tags</code> to copy every tag and <code>--platform</code> to restrict a multi-platform image (<a href="https://github.com/google/go-containerregistry/blob/main/cmd/crane/doc/crane_copy.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">crane copy</a>). Keeping the digest is what lets a deployment pin a mirror by digest. The documentation of <code>crane copy</code> mentions no flag for referrers, so signatures and attestations are not copied by it, and a tool that copies them is needed where they matter. That tool was not tested here.</p><h2 id="signatures-and-referrers" tabindex="-1">Signatures and referrers <a class="header-anchor" href="#signatures-and-referrers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Signatures and referrers&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Observed, the repository <code>stefanprodan/charts/podinfo</code> holds, next to each version tag such as <code>6.3.4</code>, a tag of the form <code>sha256-&lt;digest&gt;.sig</code>. The digest is that of the signed manifest: the manifest tagged <code>6.3.4</code> has digest <code>sha256:8f513021...</code> and the repository has a tag <code>sha256-8f513021....sig</code> whose manifest is an OCI manifest with a layer of media type <code>application/vnd.dev.cosign.simplesigning.v1+json</code> and the annotation <code>dev.cosignproject.cosign/signature</code>. So cosign signatures live in the same repository as the artifact, under a tag, and they are not separate packages. The same <code>.sig</code> tags appear in <code>cloudnative-pg/charts/cloudnative-pg</code>, <code>home-operations/charts-mirror/cilium</code> and <code>sigstore/cosign/cosign</code>.</p><p>A tag listing of such a repository therefore mixes release tags with signature tags. A cleanup script that deletes by tag must not treat <code>.sig</code> tags as releases, and must delete a signature with the version it describes.</p><p>The documentation says nothing about the OCI referrers API. Observed, <code>GET /v2/&lt;name&gt;/referrers/&lt;digest&gt;</code> on <code>stefanprodan/charts/podinfo</code> returns <code>404</code> with <code>MANIFEST_UNKNOWN</code>, both for a made-up digest and for the real digest of <code>6.3.4</code>. The OCI Distribution Specification expects a registry that implements the API to answer <code>200</code> with an empty index when nothing refers to the digest, and tells clients to fall back to a tag derived from the digest when the response is <code>404</code> (<a href="https://github.com/opencontainers/distribution-spec/blob/main/spec.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">OCI Distribution Specification</a>). So <code>ghcr.io</code> was observed not to serve the referrers API on 2026-10-16, and signatures there are found by tag, not by referrers. This is a snapshot of behavior GitHub does not document and may change.</p><h2 id="helm-charts" tabindex="-1">Helm charts <a class="header-anchor" href="#helm-charts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Helm charts&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>helm push</code> takes a reference that &quot;must be prefixed with <code>oci://</code> and must not contain the basename or tag&quot;. The basename &quot;is inferred from the chart&#39;s name&quot; and the tag &quot;is inferred from the chart&#39;s semantic version&quot;, which Helm calls &quot;a strict requirement&quot; (<a href="https://helm.sh/docs/topics/registries/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Helm: Use OCI-based registries</a>). So <code>helm push api-1.4.0.tgz oci://ghcr.io/&lt;owner&gt;/charts/shop</code> publishes <code>ghcr.io/&lt;owner&gt;/charts/shop/api:1.4.0</code>. Helm&#39;s page has no <code>ghcr.io</code> push example, and says of <code>helm registry login</code> that the host is given without a scheme or a path. The same layout is observed in public use at <code>ghcr.io/stefanprodan/charts/podinfo</code> and <code>ghcr.io/cloudnative-pg/charts/cloudnative-pg</code>.</p><p>The Container registry documentation does not mention Helm charts. It lists &quot;Open Container Initiative (OCI) Specifications&quot; as a supported format (<a href="https://docs.github.com/en/packages/working-with-a-github-packages-registry/working-with-the-container-registry" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Working with the Container registry</a>).</p>',28)])])}const u=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{l as __pageData,u as default};
